{
  "$comment": [
    "The conformance fixture. Neither implementation owns it.",
    "",
    "There are two implementations of the scorer set: agent/eval/scorers.py, which is what",
    "MLflow evaluation and production monitoring register, and shared/answer-scorers.ts, which",
    "is what the Benchmark Lab's runner executes. That duplication is forced -- the only path",
    "in this product that reaches the agent as the signed-in caller is Node, and the only place",
    "MLflow can register a scorer is Python -- and it is a real risk rather than a tidy one: a",
    "scorer that means something slightly different in production than it did in development",
    "produces a regression signal nobody can act on, because the first question is always",
    "whether the agent changed or the scorer did.",
    "",
    "So both sides read this file and assert the same verdicts against it. A change to one",
    "implementation that the other does not match fails on both sides, naming the scorer and",
    "the case. Add a case here when a scorer's behaviour is worth pinning; do not add one that",
    "only one side can satisfy.",
    "",
    "`expect` names the scorer and the verdict. `null` means the scorer must abstain, which is",
    "asserted as strictly as a value: an abstention that quietly became a pass is the failure",
    "this whole set is built to prevent.",
    "",
    "`only` marks a case one implementation is not held to. There is exactly one scorer that",
    "legitimately differs -- sql_validity, where Python asks the runtime policy through a parser",
    "and Node asserts the guarantees that policy already enforced -- and the point of writing the",
    "divergence down here is that it is now a fact of the fixture rather than a surprise in a",
    "run. Do not reach for `only` to make a failing case pass. If a verdict differs for any",
    "reason other than the one recorded on `sqlValidity`, the implementations have drifted."
  ],
